    "long_instructions": "In Lesson 5, students complete this exact task: defining a function, then calling it, the same two steps from the function example you just read about.\r\n\r\nYou’re building a music streaming program that plays a user’s favorite songs.\r\n\r\n**Do This**\r\n\r\n1. Update the definition of `play_song` to print a song of your choice to play. For example, your program might print “Now playing: Dancing Queen”.\r\n2. Call your `play_song` function.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nTo “call” a function is to tell the computer to run it. To call a function in Python, use its name followed by parentheses. For example, to call a function named `hello`, you could write:\r\n\r\n```python\r\nhello()\r\n```\r\n\r\n---\r\n**Reflection:** What might your students find challenging about this task, remembering to call the function after defining it, or something else?",
